// ============================================================================
// lib/array.bx — the operations on a growable array that every program reaches for.
//
//     use "lib/array.bx";
//
// This file could not be written until v0.0.201, and the reason is worth stating because it was
// misdiagnosed for a long time: **no function could modify an array it was passed.** Not in place, not
// by `push`. My own notes blamed generic `xs[i] = v`; that was wrong, and generics were never involved.
// A parameter was simply always a copy, and `mutable` on one was a parse error.
//
// So `sort`, `reverse`, `swap` and `fill` — every operation whose whole job is to change what it was
// given — were unwritable, and this module was a line in a plan for six versions.
//
// ---- how to read a signature here ------------------------------------------------------
//
// **`mutable xs: [T]` means the call changes YOUR array.** That is the only thing you need to check,
// and it is in the signature rather than at the call site on purpose: the same rule `mutable self`
// follows, and the reason both follow it is that a promise belongs where it is made once, not at every
// place it is used. A function without `mutable` cannot touch what you passed, and the compiler
// enforces that rather than the documentation asking nicely.
//
//     sort(mutable xs)      changes xs
//     index_of(xs, of)      cannot
//
// ---- what is NOT here, and why ---------------------------------------------------------
//
// **Sorting Strings works** (v0.0.202), by BYTE order — so "Zebra" comes before "apple", because `Z`
// is 90 and `a` is 97. That is not alphabetical order in any language, and it is deliberate: locale
// collation means picking a language and one of its several orders, which is a decision nobody wrote
// down. Byte order needs no decision and is identical on every machine, which is what a reproducible
// sort is built on.
//
// ~~**No `map` or `filter`.** Those need function values, which the language does not have.**~~
// **Both exist as of v0.0.279**, along with `fold`, `any`, `all`, `position`, `retain`, `partition`
// and `sort_by` — roadmap D2d, and see the `// ---- higher order ----` section below.
//
// The claim was true when written and the diagnosis was right: they DO need function values. What
// changed is that the language got them without getting closures. `dynamic Trait` had been a function
// value since v0.0.14; A9 made it generic, which is the whole of what was missing, and A10 (closures)
// was then closed without being built. The vocabulary lives in `lib/fn.bx` and the reasoning for its
// shape lives there too.
//
// The old advice — *"write the loop; it is three lines and a reader can see what it does"* — is still
// right for a one-off, and nothing here deprecates a `for` loop. What a named function buys is the
// case where the loop is NOT three lines: `array_sort_by` is a stable merge sort, and nobody should
// write that inline.
//
// ~~**`array_index_of` is not `pure`, and it should be.**~~ **Fixed in v0.0.248, and this comment had
// the diagnosis exactly right.** It said `Option.Some(i)` "reads as a method call on the enum" and
// that the `pure` checker "cannot see through enum construction". That was the mechanism: a variant
// constructor PARSES as a method call and is told apart inside the method-call branch, but the
// blanket *"a pure function may not call a method"* refusal sat at the TOP of that branch, before
// anything looked at whether the receiver was an enum. So a constructor was refused for being SHAPED
// like a method call.
//
// One removal fixed both halves of roadmap A4 — `pure` on a method, and `pure` returning an Option —
// which had been listed as two related items and were one branch. **A comment that names a mechanism
// rather than a symptom is worth more than a bug report**, and this one was load-bearing two years
// after it was written.
//
// ~~**An Option-returning GENERIC is not writable at all.**~~ **Fixed in v0.0.241.** `Option.None` and
// `Option.Some` now resolve their type argument from the enclosing signature, so `array_pop<T> -> Option<T>`
// compiles and runs in both compilers — measured.
//
// `min` and `max` still take a precondition, and that stays: *"the largest of nothing"* is not a
// question with a wrong answer, it is a question that should not have been asked, which is what
// `requires` says. **The limitation was never the reason for that design and the note claimed it
// was** — a real reason and a convenient excuse for it had been written down together, and only one
// of them was load-bearing.
// ============================================================================

use "option.bx";
use "fn.bx";
// D2c/D2f use `math_min` and `math_clamp`.
use "math.bx";

// ---- asking ----------------------------------------------------------------------------

// Is this value in the array? `T: Equatable` because that is exactly the promise `==` needs.
pure function array_contains<T: Equatable>(xs: [T], of: T) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if xs[i] == of {
            return true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return false;
}

// Where it first appears, or None.
//
// `Option<Int>` rather than -1, and the difference is the whole habit this language is built on: -1 is
// a valid Int, so a caller who forgets to check gets an index rather than a mistake. None cannot be
// used as an index by accident.
pure function array_index_of<T: Equatable>(xs: [T], of: T) -> Option<Int> {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if xs[i] == of {
            return Option.Some(i);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Option.None;
}

pure function array_is_sorted<T: Ordered>(xs: [T]) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if xs[i] < xs[i - 1] {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// The smallest and largest. A PRECONDITION rather than an Option, and the reason is the better one
// rather than the convenient one: "the largest of nothing" is not a question with a wrong answer, it
// is a question that should not have been asked — which is exactly what `requires` says. The same call
// `vector_normalise` makes about the zero vector.
//
// (There is also a language reason, and it is worth recording because it is a real gap rather than a
// preference: `Option.None` could not be built where `T` was a type PARAMETER, and even
// `let nothing: Option<T> = Option.None;` was refused. **Both work as of v0.0.241** — the claim about
// the `let` was TRUE when written and was verified before being retired rather than assumed stale.
//
// The cause turned out to be inverted from how it read: it was not that methods had this inference and
// free functions lacked it. **A free generic function's body is checked ABSTRACTLY, and a generic
// method's body never is** — `lib/map.bx`'s `find -> Option<V>` has shipped since v0.0.118 not because
// methods resolve it correctly but because they never enter the state that broke free functions. There
// was no working path to copy.)
function array_min<T: Ordered>(xs: [T]) -> T
    requires len(xs) > 0
{
    let mutable best: T = xs[0];
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if xs[i] < best {
            best = xs[i];
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return best;
}

function array_max<T: Ordered>(xs: [T]) -> T
    requires len(xs) > 0
{
    let mutable best: T = xs[0];
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if best < xs[i] {
            best = xs[i];
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return best;
}

function array_first<T>(xs: [T]) -> T
    requires len(xs) > 0
{
    return xs[0];
}

function array_last<T>(xs: [T]) -> T
    requires len(xs) > 0
{
    return xs[len(xs) - 1];
}

// A total, and it TRAPS on overflow rather than wrapping — which is the same promise `+` makes, kept
// here rather than quietly relaxed because this is a loop. A total that silently wrapped would be the
// exact wrong answer this language exists to refuse, and a sum is where money lives.
//
// Two of them rather than one generic, because `0` and `$0.00` are different values and a generic has
// no way to name "the zero of T" — there is no `Default`, deliberately.
pure function array_sum_int(xs: [Int]) -> Int {
    let mutable total: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        total = total + xs[i];
        i += 1;
    }
    return total;
}

pure function array_sum_money(xs: [Decimal<2>]) -> Decimal<2> {
    let mutable total: Decimal<2> = $0.00;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        total = total + xs[i];
        i += 1;
    }
    return total;
}

// ---- changing --------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Everything below takes `mutable xs`, so a reader of the CALL knows the array changes. That is what
// this module was waiting for.

// Exchange two elements. The one operation `sort` and `reverse` are both built from, so it is worth
// having by name — and the precondition is not defensive: an index outside the array has no element
// to exchange, and answering anyway is how a swap silently corrupts a neighbour.
function array_swap<T>(mutable xs: [T], i: Int, j: Int) -> Int
    requires i >= 0
    requires i < len(xs)
    requires j >= 0
    requires j < len(xs)
{
    let held: T = xs[i];
    xs[i] = xs[j];
    xs[j] = held;
    return len(xs);
}

function array_reverse<T>(mutable xs: [T]) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    let mutable j: Int = len(xs) - 1;
    while i < j {
        let held: T = xs[i];
        xs[i] = xs[j];
        xs[j] = held;
        i += 1;
        j -= 1;
    }
    return len(xs);
}

// Insertion sort, and the choice is deliberate rather than lazy.
//
// It is **stable** — equal elements keep the order they were written in — which for records sorted by
// one field is the difference between a reproducible listing and one that reshuffles between runs. It
// needs **no extra storage**, so it does not allocate and can be called from a function that declares
// `allocates nothing`. And it is **O(n) on an already-sorted array**, which is what a program that
// sorts after every append actually does.
//
// It is O(n²) on reversed input, and that is the honest cost. A merge sort would need a scratch array
// and a way to allocate one; a quicksort would lose stability and need a pivot choice nobody wrote
// down. When a corpus arrives that this is too slow for, the answer is a second named function rather
// than quietly changing this one, because a caller may be depending on the stability.
function array_sort<T: Ordered>(mutable xs: [T]) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    while i < len(xs) {
        let mutable j: Int = i;
        // `xs[j] < xs[j - 1]` and not `<=`: on EQUAL elements the loop stops, which is what makes
        // this stable. Swapping equals would reverse their original order for no reason.
        while j > 0 && xs[j] < xs[j - 1] {
            let held: T = xs[j];
            xs[j] = xs[j - 1];
            xs[j - 1] = held;
            j -= 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return len(xs);
}

// Every element set to one value. Keeps the length: this fills what is there rather than growing.
function array_fill<T>(mutable xs: [T], with: T) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        xs[i] = with;
        i += 1;
    }
    return len(xs);
}

// Append every element of `from` to `xs`. `from` is NOT mutable, and the asymmetry is the point: one
// of these two arrays changes, and the signature says which.
function array_extend<T>(mutable xs: [T], from: [T]) -> Int {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(from) {
        let added: Int = push(xs, from[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    return len(xs);
}

// Remove the element at `at`, closing the gap. Order is preserved, which is why this shifts rather
// than swapping the last element into the hole — a faster removal that reorders is a different
// function and would need a different name to say so.
function array_remove_at<T>(mutable xs: [T], at: Int) -> Int
    requires at >= 0
    requires at < len(xs)
{
    let mutable i: Int = at;
    while i < len(xs) - 1 {
        xs[i] = xs[i + 1];
        i += 1;
    }
    let shorter: Int = truncate(xs, len(xs) - 1);
    return len(xs);
}

// ---- copying and reshaping (roadmap D1e) -----------------------------------------------

// A NEW array with the same elements.
//
// **Read this one before you assume you do not need it.** Assigning an array does NOT copy it:
//
//     let mutable b: [Int] = a;
//     b[0] = 99;                    // a[0] is now 99
//
// That is measured, in both compilers, not inferred from the memory model. An array value carries a
// pointer to its buffer, and assignment copies the value, so both names see one buffer. Every
// function in this file that answers a new array therefore builds it with `push` rather than starting
// from an assignment — and a caller who wants an independent array has to say so, which is what this
// is for.
//
// It is not a deep copy. If `T` is a class, the elements are still shared; there is no way to write a
// deep copy generically, because there is no interface saying how to clone a `T`.
function array_copy<T>(xs: [T]) -> [T] {
    let mutable out: [T] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        push(out, xs[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// The elements from `from` up to but NOT including `to`, as a new array.
//
// End-EXCLUSIVE, matching `string_slice` and the reason it chose the same: `to - from` is the length,
// `array_slice(xs, 0, len(xs))` is the whole thing, and two adjacent slices meet without an
// off-by-one at the seam. Inclusive ends read more naturally in one call and wrongly in every
// composition of two.
//
// An empty slice is legal — `from == to` answers `[]` — because a loop that narrows a range down to
// nothing should not have to special-case its last step. `from > to` is not legal, because that is a
// reversed range rather than an empty one, and answering `[]` would hide the caller's arithmetic bug.
function array_slice<T>(xs: [T], from: Int, to: Int) -> [T]
    requires from >= 0
    requires to >= from
    requires to <= len(xs)
{
    let mutable out: [T] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = from;
    while i < to {
        push(out, xs[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// Both arrays' elements, in order, as a NEW one. Neither argument changes — which is the difference
// between this and `array_extend`, and the reason both exist.
function array_concat<T>(first: [T], second: [T]) -> [T] {
    let mutable out: [T] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(first) {
        push(out, first[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    let mutable j: Int = 0;
    while j < len(second) {
        push(out, second[j]);
        j += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// Put `value` at `at`, shifting everything from there along. `at == len(xs)` appends, which is why the
// precondition is `<=` and not `<` — inserting at the end is the ordinary case of a loop that inserts
// in order, not an edge to be refused.
function array_insert_at<T>(mutable xs: [T], at: Int, value: T) -> Int
    requires at >= 0
    requires at <= len(xs)
{
    push(xs, value);                 // grow by one; if `at` is the end this is already the answer
    let mutable i: Int = len(xs) - 1;
    while i > at {
        xs[i] = xs[i - 1];
        i -= 1;
    }
    xs[at] = value;
    return len(xs);
}

// How many times a value appears. `array_contains` asks whether it appears at all; this is the count,
// and having both means neither caller writes the loop.
pure function array_count_of<T: Equatable>(xs: [T], of: T) -> Int {
    let mutable seen: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if xs[i] == of {
            seen += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return seen;
}

// Same length and equal elements in the same order.
//
// Explicit rather than `a == b`, because `==` on two arrays is not defined and this says what the
// comparison IS: order-sensitive, so `[1, 2]` and `[2, 1]` differ. A set comparison is `lib/set.bx`.
pure function array_equals<T: Equatable>(first: [T], second: [T]) -> Bool {
    if len(first) != len(second) {
        return false;
    }
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(first) {
        if !(first[i] == second[i]) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// The index of `of`, found in O(log n) — or None.
//
// **The array must be sorted ascending, and that is a requirement on the CALLER rather than a
// `requires` clause.** The reason is cost and it is worth being explicit about, because omitting a
// precondition is normally the wrong call in this language: `requires array_is_sorted(xs)` is O(n),
// which would make an O(log n) function linear and leave a caller no faster than `array_index_of`.
// A contract that costs more than the function it guards is a contract nobody can afford to keep.
//
// On an unsorted array this answers *something* — possibly None for a value that is present. That is
// the honest cost of the above. `array_index_of` is the one to reach for when sortedness is not
// already known, and `array_is_sorted` exists if you want to check once outside a loop.
//
// **On duplicates it answers the FIRST match**, not an arbitrary one. A plain binary search may stop
// on any of them, which makes the result depend on the length of the array rather than its contents —
// reproducible in the sense that it is deterministic, useless in the sense that inserting an unrelated
// element changes it. So this is a lower-bound search followed by one equality test: same O(log n),
// one answer.
pure function array_binary_search<T: Ordered>(xs: [T], of: T) -> Option<Int> {
    let mutable low: Int = 0;
    let mutable high: Int = len(xs);                 // exclusive
    // Lower bound: on exit `low` is the first index whose element is not before `of`.
    // `low + divide_floor(high - low, 2)` rather than `divide_floor(low + high, 2)`, which can
    // overflow — and `+` TRAPS here, so that would be a crash rather than a wrong answer. Still
    // worth writing correctly: a trap on a 4-billion-element array is not a good failure either.
    while low < high {
        let mid: Int = low + divide_floor(high - low, 2);
        if xs[mid] < of {
            low = mid + 1;
        } else {
            high = mid;
        }
    }
    // `low` has `!(xs[low] < of)`. If also `!(of < xs[low])` then neither is before the other, which
    // for an Ordered type is equality — expressed with `<` alone, because `Ordered` promises `<` and
    // a second `T: Equatable` bound would narrow who may call this for no gain.
    if low < len(xs) && !(of < xs[low]) {
        return Option.Some(low);
    }
    return Option.None;
}

// Drop ADJACENT duplicates, in place, answering the new length.
//
// **Adjacent, which means this only removes ALL duplicates from a SORTED array.** On `[1, 2, 1]`
// nothing is removed, and that is not a bug — it is `uniq`, and it is the version that runs in O(n)
// with no allocation and no `Ordered` bound. It is also exactly the shape of function this project
// keeps getting wrong: right about the sorted case somebody wrote, silent about the unsorted case
// nobody did. So it is said here, and `tests/pass/array_higher_order.bx` pins `[1, 2, 1]`
// specifically so the behaviour cannot drift into the other one unnoticed.
//
// For all-distinct regardless of order: `array_sort` then this, or `lib/set.bx`, which keeps
// insertion order and costs a hash per element.
function array_dedup<T: Equatable>(mutable xs: [T]) -> Int {
    if len(xs) < 2 {
        return len(xs);
    }
    let mutable kept: Int = 1;                       // xs[0] always survives
    let mutable i: Int = 1;
    let count: Int = len(xs);                        // hoisted: the array shrinks under us at the end
    while i < count {
        if !(xs[i] == xs[kept - 1]) {
            xs[kept] = xs[i];
            kept += 1;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    let shorter: Int = truncate(xs, kept);
    return kept;
}

// ---- higher order (roadmap D2d) --------------------------------------------------------
//
// `map`, `filter`, `fold` and the rest, over the interfaces in `lib/fn.bx`. Read that file's header
// once: it says how a caller writes an implementation, why it takes two `let`s rather than one, and
// what state-as-a-field looks like next to a closure.
//
// **Nothing in this section is `pure`, and every one of them looks like it should be.** `array_any`
// reads an array and answers a Bool; so does `array_contains`, which IS pure. The difference is that
// this one's answer is only as pure as the predicate it was handed, and the compiler currently lets a
// `pure` function call through a `dynamic` to arbitrary code — measured in both compilers, written up
// in `lib/fn.bx`. Marking these `pure` would compile and would be a claim the language cannot check.
//
// **On cost, measured rather than assumed — and the answer was not the expected one.** One vtable
// indirection per element sounds like it should show up, so it was measured against the identical loop
// with the operation written inline and no interface at all:
//
//     array_map, 100,000 Ints          dynamic  5,104 KB   inline  5,104 KB    (identical)
//     array_map, 5,000,000 Ints        dynamic    210 MB   inline    210 MB    0.07 s both
//     array_fold, 5,000,000 Ints       dynamic  0.04 s     inline  0.04 s      (no allocation
//                                                                               in the loop, so the
//                                                                               call is the largest
//                                                                               remaining term)
//
// **Zero memory cost, and no measurable time cost.** The memory result is not luck and is the part
// that generalises: an interface object is two words, `{data, vtable}`, and the data half IS the
// storage of the variable it was coerced from — `DynCoerce` copies nothing. So there is no per-element
// allocation and no closure to heap, which is the cost a closure-based `map` would have had.
//
// The time result is the weaker claim of the two and worth stating narrowly: it says the indirection
// is not the bottleneck in a loop that also touches memory per element, not that an indirect call is
// free in the abstract. A tight arithmetic loop over data already in cache could still show it.

// Every element through `f`, as a new array. `[T]` in, `[U]` out — so this is the type-changing form,
// and `Mapper<T, T>` covers the case where it does not change.
function array_map<T, U>(xs: [T], f: dynamic Mapper<T, U>) -> [U] {
    let mutable out: [U] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        push(out, f.apply(xs[i]));
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// The elements that pass, as a new array, in their original order.
function array_filter<T>(xs: [T], keep: dynamic Predicate<T>) -> [T] {
    let mutable out: [T] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if keep.test(xs[i]) {
            push(out, xs[i]);
            i += 1;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return out;
}

// Left fold: `start`, then one `step` per element, in order.
//
// **`start` is a parameter and there is no version without one**, which is not an omission. A
// `fold` that used "the zero of T" would need a `Default` interface, and this language deliberately
// has none — `array_sum_int` and `array_sum_money` are two functions rather than one generic for
// exactly that reason (`0` and `$0.00` are different values and nothing can name both). Passing the
// start also makes the empty case total: the fold of nothing is `start`, no precondition needed.
//
// Left rather than right, and only left. A right fold over an array is a left fold over its reverse,
// and `array_reverse` is right there; shipping both would mean a reader checking which one they have.
function array_fold<T, A>(xs: [T], start: A, f: dynamic Folder<T, A>) -> A {
    let mutable held: A = start;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        held = f.step(held, xs[i]);
        i += 1;
    }
    return held;
}

// Does any element pass? False for an empty array, and it stops at the first one that does.
function array_any<T>(xs: [T], holds: dynamic Predicate<T>) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if holds.test(xs[i]) {
            return true;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return false;
}

// Do all of them pass? **True for an empty array** — vacuously, because there is no element that
// fails. That answer surprises people often enough to be worth a line: it is what makes
// `array_all(a, p) && array_all(b, p)` equal `array_all(concat(a, b), p)` for every a and b, and any
// other choice breaks that. Pinned in the fixture.
function array_all<T>(xs: [T], holds: dynamic Predicate<T>) -> Bool {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if !holds.test(xs[i]) {
            return false;
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return true;
}

// The index of the first element that passes, or None. The predicate counterpart of
// `array_index_of`, and `Option<Int>` for the same reason that one gives: -1 is a usable index and
// None is not.
function array_position<T>(xs: [T], holds: dynamic Predicate<T>) -> Option<Int> {
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if holds.test(xs[i]) {
            return Option.Some(i);
        }
        i += 1;
    }
    return Option.None;
}

// Keep the elements that pass, IN PLACE, answering the new length.
//
// `array_filter`'s mutating twin, and the signature is the whole difference: `mutable xs` says the
// call changes your array, where `array_filter` cannot touch it. Having both is the point — a filter
// into a new array is what you want inside an expression, and a retain is what you want when the
// array is large and the copy is the expensive part.
//
// Compacting rather than repeated removal: one pass, each survivor moved down to the next open slot,
// then one truncate. Removing in place with `array_remove_at` would be O(n²) — it shifts the whole
// tail per removal — which for a predicate that rejects most elements is the difference between a
// scan and a stall.
function array_retain<T>(mutable xs: [T], keep: dynamic Predicate<T>) -> Int {
    let mutable kept: Int = 0;
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    // Hoisted, because `truncate` at the end changes what `len(xs)` answers — and because the
    // predicate is arbitrary code, so re-reading the length every step would be re-reading it after
    // something that had no business changing it.
    let count: Int = len(xs);
    while i < count {
        if keep.test(xs[i]) {
            xs[kept] = xs[i];
            kept += 1;
            i += 1;
        } else {
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    let shorter: Int = truncate(xs, kept);
    return kept;
}

// Split into those that pass and those that do not: `(passing, failing)`, both new arrays, each in
// original order.
//
// A TUPLE return, which A8 landed the same day as A9 — before that this needed a two-field class
// declared somewhere a caller could reach, which is precisely the record nobody wants to invent.
// `.0` is the passing half; the order is the same as `filter` first, which is the reading order of
// the name.
//
// One pass rather than two calls to `array_filter` with opposite predicates: a caller cannot write
// the negation of an arbitrary `Predicate` without declaring another class, and calling the predicate
// twice per element would double the vtable cost for an answer already in hand.
function array_partition<T>(xs: [T], keep: dynamic Predicate<T>) -> ([T], [T])
{
    let mutable passing: [T] = [];
    let mutable failing: [T] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        if keep.test(xs[i]) {
            push(passing, xs[i]);
            i += 1;
        } else {
            push(failing, xs[i]);
            i += 1;
        }
    }
    return (passing, failing);
}

// Sort in place by a caller's order. **STABLE**: elements the comparer calls neither-before-the-other
// keep the order they were in.
//
// ---- which algorithm, and why it is not the one above ----------------------------------
//
// **Bottom-up merge sort**, where `array_sort` is insertion sort. Two different answers to two
// different questions, and the deciding factor is one this file did not have before today:
//
// **Every comparison here is a vtable call**, where `array_sort` compares with `<` on an `Ordered`
// type and the compiler emits it inline. So the COUNT of comparisons stopped being a secondary
// concern. **Measured**, reversed input, this merge sort against insertion sort with the same
// comparer — the version this function would be if it had simply copied `array_sort`'s algorithm:
//
//     n           this (merge)      insertion sort with a comparer
//     20,000      0.00 s              0.28 s
//     40,000      0.00 s              1.15 s
//     80,000      0.00 s              4.99 s
//     2,000,000   0.14 s              not attempted
//
// Insertion sort quadruples for every doubling, which is the O(n²) showing up exactly where it
// should. The last row is the one that settles it: this sorts **25 times as many elements in a
// thirtieth of the time** that insertion sort needs for 80,000. This is the *"a faster stable sort"*
// roadmap D1e asked for, arriving with the feature that made a comparison expensive enough to need it.
//
// **`array_sort` stays and is still the right call** for an `Ordered` type: its `<` is inline, so none
// of the above applies to it, and it allocates nothing — so it can be called where a scratch array
// cannot be had. Two functions, and the choice is visible in which one you typed.
//
// **Why merge and not quicksort**, given quicksort also gets O(n log n) with no allocation: quicksort
// is not stable, and stability is the promise in this function's first line. It also needs a pivot
// rule, and every simple pivot rule has an input shape that makes it quadratic — which for a language
// arguing about reproducibility is a worse failure than the scratch array.
//
// **The cost, stated:** one scratch array of `n` elements, and `n` writes back per pass — so
// `n log n` copies on top of `n log n` comparisons. Ping-ponging between the two buffers would halve
// the copying, but the result has to END in `xs`, so it would depend on the number of passes being
// even and need a final conditional copy. Not worth the reasoning for a constant factor on the cheap
// half of the loop; the expensive half is the comparisons, and those are already minimal.
function array_sort_by<T>(mutable xs: [T], order: dynamic Comparer<T>) -> Int {
    let count: Int = len(xs);
    if count < 2 {
        return count;
    }

    // Element by element. `let mutable scratch: [T] = xs;` would ALIAS — both names would be one
    // buffer, and the merge would overwrite the input it was reading. See `array_copy`, where that is
    // measured rather than argued.
    let mutable scratch: [T] = [];
    let mutable c: Int = 0;
    while c < count {
        push(scratch, xs[c]);
        c += 1;
    }

    // Runs of 1 are sorted by definition, so the first pass merges pairs, then pairs of pairs.
    let mutable width: Int = 1;
    while width < count {
        let mutable low: Int = 0;
        while low < count {
            let mutable mid: Int = low + width;
            if mid > count {
                mid = count;
            }
            let mutable high: Int = low + width + width;
            if high > count {
                high = count;
            }

            // Merge xs[low..mid) and xs[mid..high) into scratch[low..high).
            let mutable left: Int = low;
            let mutable right: Int = mid;
            let mutable at: Int = low;
            while at < high {
                if left < mid && right < high {
                    // **This branch is the stability**, and it is the only place it lives. Take from
                    // the right run ONLY when its element is STRICTLY before the left one; on
                    // neither-before-the-other, take the left. The left run holds the elements that
                    // came first, so equals come out in the order they went in. `order.before(left,
                    // right)` with the branches swapped would look equivalent and would reverse
                    // every group of equals — which is the defect the fixture's `by_length`
                    // comparer over same-length words exists to catch.
                    if order.before(xs[right], xs[left]) {
                        scratch[at] = xs[right];
                        right += 1;
                    } else {
                        scratch[at] = xs[left];
                        left += 1;
                    }
                } else {
                    if left < mid {
                        scratch[at] = xs[left];
                        left += 1;
                    } else {
                        scratch[at] = xs[right];
                        right += 1;
                    }
                }
                at += 1;
            }

            low = low + width + width;
        }

        let mutable back: Int = 0;
        while back < count {
            xs[back] = scratch[back];
            back += 1;
        }
        width = width + width;
    }
    return count;
}

// ---- pairs and pieces (roadmap D2c and D2f) -------------------------------------------------
//
// These waited on tuples (A8), which is why they are here rather than beside `array_map`: `zip`
// answers pairs, and before A8 there was nowhere for a pair to live that was not a class somebody
// had to declare first.
//
// **Everything below COPIES, and that is the slicing decision D2f was waiting for, made and
// written down rather than left open.** Burxt has no borrowed sub-slice: a value is either its own
// storage or a whole array. So `array_chunks` builds real arrays, and the cost is one copy of every
// element — the same total work as the walk that produced them, and O(n) rather than O(1) per piece.
//
// The alternative was a borrowed view, and it is refused for the reason the region model refuses
// most references: a view outlives nothing safely without a lifetime to check it against, and the
// language deliberately has none. A copy is honest, and a caller who cannot afford it should index
// the original — which is what the loop they were about to write does anyway.

// The two arrays walked together, stopping at the shorter. `zip([1,2,3], ["a","b"])` has two pairs.
//
// Stopping at the shorter rather than refusing unequal lengths: the caller who wants them equal has
// `array_equals` on the lengths and a clearer error than this could give, and the caller who does
// not want them equal is the common one — walking a list against its first N labels.
function array_zip<A, B>(left: [A], right: [B]) -> [(A, B)] {
    let mutable out: [(A, B)] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    let stop: Int = math_min(len(left), len(right));
    while i < stop {
        let paired: Int = push(out, (left[i], right[i]));
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// Every element with its index. `enumerate(["a","b"])` is `[(0,"a"), (1,"b")]`.
function array_enumerate<T>(xs: [T]) -> [(Int, T)] {
    let mutable out: [(Int, T)] = [];
    let mutable i: Int = 0;
    while i < len(xs) {
        let paired: Int = push(out, (i, xs[i]));
        i += 1;
    }
    return out;
}

// **`array_split_at` is NOT here, and it was written, tested, and taken out again.** §B54.
//
// It works in stage-0. Under stage-1 it SEGFAULTS when the element is a String — `[Int]` is fine —
// and only from inside this file: the same function copied into a standalone program works, and so
// does the `halve<T>(xs) -> ([T], [T])` shape that `tests/pass/a_tuple_of_slices_in_a_generic.bx`
// already covers. So the trigger needs this file's other instantiations to be present, which is why
// no existing fixture found it.
//
// That existing fixture is the whole lesson: it uses `[Int]`. A tuple of two slices from a generic
// was covered, and covered for ONE element type, so the defect sat behind a green suite — the same
// shape as B52, B18, B35 and B42.
//
// Shipping it with a note would have been worse than not shipping it: a library function that
// crashes under one of two compilers is not a library function. It comes back when B54 closes.

// **`array_chunks` and `array_windows` are absent, and D2f is not what its row assumed.**
//
// The row files them under "needs a slicing decision — a borrowed sub-slice or a copy". That
// decision was easy and it is COPY, for the reason the region model refuses most references: a view
// outlives nothing safely without a lifetime to check it against, and this language deliberately
// has none.
//
// That is not the blocker. Measured v0.0.294, writing the obvious version:
//
//     error: a growable array cannot hold another array yet — its element would need its own region
//
// **`[[T]]` does not exist.** Both functions answer a list of lists, so neither is writable at all
// until a growable array can hold one — which is a language feature rather than a library choice,
// and a bigger question than either function: an element that is itself a region needs somewhere to
// live that outlasts the `push`.
//
// Written down here rather than left as an absence, because the next person to reach for `chunks`
// would otherwise spend an afternoon rediscovering it. Index the original array — the loop that
// does it is the loop `chunks` would have run for you.
